L2261@P71: The required behavior for the Write-Back Read and Write-Allocate memory type is the same as for Write-Back
L2262@P71: No-Allocate memory. But in this case the allocation hint is that, for performance reasons:
L2263@P71: • Allocation of read transactions is recommended.
L2264@P71-72: • Allocation of write transactions is recommended.A4 Transaction Attributes
L2265@P72: A4.5 Mismatched memory attributes
L2266@P72: ARM IHI 0022H Copyright © 2003-2020 Arm Limited or its affiliates. All rights reserved. A4-73
L2267@P72: ID040120 Non-Confidential
L2268@P72: A4.5 Mismatched memory attributes
L2269@P72: Multiple agents that are accessing the same area of memory, can use mismatched memory attributes. However, for
L2270@P72: functional correctness, the following rules must be obeyed:
L2271@P72: • All masters accessing the same area of memory must have a consistent view of the cacheability of that area
L2272@P72: of memory at any level of hierarchy. The rules to be applied are:
L2273@P72: Address region not Cacheable
L2274@P72: All masters must use transactions with both AxCACHE[3:2] deasserted.
L2275@P72: Address region Cacheable
L2276@P72: All masters must use transactions with either of AxCACHE[3:2] asserted.
L2277@P72: • Different masters can use different allocation hints.
L2278@P72: • If an addressed region is Normal Non-cacheable, any master can access it using a Device memory
L2279@P72: transaction.
L2280@P72: • If an addressed region has the Bufferable attribute, any master can access it using transactions that do not
L2281@P72: permit bufferable behavior.
L2282@P72: Note
L2283@P72:  For example, a transaction that requires the response from the final destination does not permit bufferable
L2284@P72: behavior.
L2285@P72: A4.5.1 Changing memory attributes
L2286@P72: The attributes for a particular memory region can be changed from one type to another incompatible type. For
L2287@P72: example, the attribute can be changed from Write-Through Cacheable to Normal Non-cacheable. This change
L2288@P72: requires a suitable process to perform the change. Typically, the following process is performed:
L2289@P72: 1. All masters stop accessing the region.
L2290@P72: 2. A single master performs any required cache maintenance operations.
L2291@P72-73: 3. All masters restart accessing the memory region, using the new attributes.A4 Transaction Attributes
L2292@P73: A4.6 Transaction buffering
L2293@P73: A4-74 Copyright © 2003-2020 Arm Limited or its affiliates. All rights reserved. ARM IHI 0022H
L2294@P73: Non-Confidential ID040120
L2295@P73: A4.6 Transaction buffering
L2296@P73: Write access to the following memory types do not require a transaction response from the final destination, but do
L2297@P73: require that write transactions are made visible at the final destination in a timely manner:
L2298@P73: • Device Bufferable
L2299@P73: • Normal Non-cacheable Bufferable
L2300@P73: • Write-Through
L2301@P73: For write transactions, all three memory types require the same behavior. For read transactions, the required
L2302@P73: behavior is as follows:
L2303@P73: • For Device Bufferable memory, read data must be obtained from the final destination.
L2304@P73: • For Normal Non-cacheable Bufferable memory, read data must be obtained either from the final destination
L2305@P73: or from a write transaction that is progressing to its final destination.
L2306@P73: • For Write-Through memory, read data can be obtained from an intermediate cached copy.
L2307@P73: In addition to ensuring that write transactions progress towards their final destination in a timely manner,
L2308@P73: intermediate buffers must behave as follows:
L2309@P73: • An intermediate buffer that can respond to a transaction must ensure that, over time, any read transaction to
L2310@P73: Normal Non-cacheable Bufferable propagates towards its destination. This propagation means that, when
L2311@P73: forwarding a read transaction, the attempted forwarding must not continue indefinitely, and any data that is
L2312@P73: used for forwarding must not persist indefinitely. The protocol does not define any mechanism for
L2313@P73: determining how long data that is used for forwarding a read transaction can persist. However, in such a
L2314@P73: mechanism, the act of reading the data must not reset the data timeout period.
L2315@P73: Note
L2316@P73:  Without this requirement, continued polling of the same location can prevent the timeout of a read that is held
L2317@P73: in the buffer, preventing the read progressing towards its destination.
L2318@P73: • An intermediate buffer that can hold and merge write transactions must ensure that transactions do not remain
L2319@P73: in its buffer indefinitely. For example, merging write transactions must not reset the mechanism that
L2320@P73: determines when a write is drained towards its final destination.
L2321@P73: Note
L2322@P73:  Without this requirement, continued writes to the same location can prevent the timeout of a write held in the
L2323@P73: buffer, preventing the write progressing towards its destination.
L2324@P73: For information about the required behavior of read accesses to these memory types, see:
L2325@P73: • Device Bufferable on page A4-70
L2326@P73: • Normal Non-cacheable Bufferable on page A4-70
L2327@P73-74: • Write-Through No-Allocate on page A4-71A4 Transaction Attributes
L2328@P74: A4.7 Access permissions
L2329@P74: ARM IHI 0022H Copyright © 2003-2020 Arm Limited or its affiliates. All rights reserved. A4-75
L2330@P74: ID040120 Non-Confidential
L2331@P74: A4.7 Access permissions
L2332@P74: AXI provides access permissions signals that can be used to protect against illegal transactions:
L2333@P74: • ARPROT[2:0] defines the access permissions for read accesses.
L2334@P74: • AWPROT[2:0] defines the access permissions for write accesses.
L2335@P74: The term AxPROT refers collectively to the ARPROT and AWPROT signals.
L2336@P74: Table A4-6 shows the AxPROT[2:0] encoding.
L2337@P74: The protection attributes are:
L2338@P74: Unprivileged or privileged
L2339@P74: An AXI master might support more than one level of operating privilege, and extend this concept
L2340@P74: of privilege to memory access. AxPROT[0] identifies an access as unprivileged or privileged.
L2341@P74: Note
L2342@P74:  Some processors support multiple levels of privilege, see the documentation for the selected
L2343@P74: processor to determine the mapping to AXI privilege levels. The only distinction AXI can provide
L2344@P74: is between privileged and unprivileged access.
L2345@P74: Secure or Non-secure
L2346@P74: An AXI master might support Secure and Non-secure operating states, and extend this concept of
L2347@P74: security to memory access. AxPROT[1] identifies an access as Secure or Non-secure. AxPROT[1]
L2348@P74: can be considered as defining two address spaces, a Secure address space and a Non-secure address
L2349@P74: space. This signal can be treated as an additional address bit. Any aliasing between the Secure and
L2350@P74: Non-secure address spaces must be handled correctly.
L2351@P74: Note
L2352@P74:  This bit is defined so that when it is asserted the transaction is identified as Non-secure. This
L2353@P74: definition is consistent with other signaling in implementations of the Arm Security Extensions.
L2354@P74: Instruction or data
L2355@P74: This bit indicates that the transaction is an instruction access or a data access.
L2356@P74: The AXI protocol defines this indication as a hint. It is not accurate in all cases, for example, where
L2357@P74: a transaction contains a mix of instruction and data items. This specification recommends that a
L2358@P74: master sets AxPROT[2] LOW, to indicate a data access unless the access is known to be an
L2359@P74: instruction access.
L2360@P74: Table A4-6 Protection encoding
L2361@P74: AxPROT Value Function
L2362@P74: [0] 0 Unprivileged access
L2363@P74: 1 Privileged access
L2364@P74: [1] 0 Secure access
L2365@P74: 1 Non-secure access
L2366@P74: [2] 0 Data access
L2367@P74-75: 1 Instruction accessA4 Transaction Attributes
L2368@P75: A4.8 Legacy considerations
L2369@P75: A4-76 Copyright © 2003-2020 Arm Limited or its affiliates. All rights reserved. ARM IHI 0022H
L2370@P75: Non-Confidential ID040120
L2371@P75: A4.8 Legacy considerations
L2372@P75: AXI4 introduces additional requirements for the handling of some of the AxCACHE memory attributes.
L2373@P75: In AXI4, all Device transactions using the same ID to the same slave must be ordered with respect to each other.
L2374@P75: Note
L2375@P75:  • This ordering is not an explicit requirement of AXI3. Any AXI4 component that relies on this behavior
L2376@P75: cannot be connected to an AXI3 interconnect that does not exhibit this behavior.
L2377@P75: • Arm believes that most implemented AXI3 interconnects support the required AXI4 behavior.
L2378@P75: This specification strongly recommends that any new AXI3 design implements the AXI4 requirement.
L2379@P75: For AxCACHE bits names and memory type names, it is required that AXI4 uses the new terms. AXI3 components
L2380@P75-76: can use either the AXI3 or AXI4 names.A4 Transaction Attributes
L2381@P76: A4.9 Usage examples
L2382@P76: ARM IHI 0022H Copyright © 2003-2020 Arm Limited or its affiliates. All rights reserved. A4-77
L2383@P76: ID040120 Non-Confidential
L2384@P76: A4.9 Usage examples
L2385@P76: This section gives examples of memory type usage.
L2386@P76: A4.9.1 Use of Device memory types
L2387@P76: The specification supports the combined use of Device Non-bufferable and Device Bufferable memory types to
L2388@P76: force write transactions to reach their final destination and ensure that the issuing master knows when the
L2389@P76: transaction is visible to all other masters.
L2390@P76: A write transaction that is marked as Device Bufferable is required to reach its final destination in a timely manner.
L2391@P76: However, the write response for the transaction can be signaled by an intermediate buffer. Therefore, the issuing
L2392@P76: master cannot know when the write is visible to all other masters.
L2393@P76: If a master issues a Device Bufferable write transaction, or stream of write transactions, followed by a Device
L2394@P76: Non-bufferable write transaction, and all transactions use the same AXI ID, the AXI ordering requirements force
L2395@P76: all of the Device Bufferable write transactions to reach the final destination before a response is given to the Device
L2396@P76: Non-bufferable transaction. Therefore, the response to the Device Non-bufferable transaction indicates that all the
L2397@P76: transactions are visible to all masters.
L2398@P76: Note
L2399@P76:  A Device Non-bufferable transaction can only guarantee the completion of Device Bufferable transactions that are
L2400@P76-77: issued with the same ID, and are to the same slave device.A4 Transaction Attributes
L2401@P77: A4.9 Usage examples
L2402@P77: A4-78 Copyright © 2003-2020 Arm Limited or its affiliates. All rights reserved. ARM IHI 0022H
L2403@P77-78: Non-Confidential ID040120ARM IHI 0022H Copyright © 2003-2020 Arm Limited or its affiliates. All rights reserved. A5-79
L2404@P78: ID040120 Non-Confidential
L2405@P78: Chapter A5
L2406@P78: Transaction Identifiers
L2407@P78: This chapter describes the mechanism that enables out-of-order transaction completion and the issuing of multiple
L2408@P78: outstanding addresses. It contains the following sections:
L2409@P78: • AXI transaction identifiers on page A5-80
L2410@P78-79: • ID signals on page A5-81A5 Transaction Identifiers
L2411@P79: A5.1 AXI transaction identifiers
L2412@P79: A5-80 Copyright © 2003-2020 Arm Limited or its affiliates. All rights reserved. ARM IHI 0022H
L2413@P79: Non-Confidential ID040120
L2414@P79: A5.1 AXI transaction identifiers
L2415@P79: The AXI protocol includes AXI ID transaction identifiers. A master can use these to identify separate transactions
L2416@P79: that must be returned in order.
L2417@P79: All transactions with a given AXI ID value must remain ordered, but there is no restriction on the ordering of
L2418@P79: transactions with different ID values. A single physical port can support out-of-order transactions by acting as a
L2419@P79: number of logical ports, each handling its transactions in order.
L2420@P79: By using AXI IDs, a master can issue transactions without waiting for earlier transactions to complete. This can
L2421@P79: improve system performance, because it enables parallel processing of transactions.
L2422@P79: Note
L2423@P79:  There is no requirement for slaves or masters to use AXI transaction IDs. Masters and slaves can process one
L2424@P79: transaction at a time. Transactions are processed in the order they are issued.
L2425@P79-80: Slaves are required to reflect on the appropriate BID or RID response an AXI ID received from a master.A5 Transaction Identifiers
L2426@P80: A5.2 ID signals
L2427@P80: ARM IHI 0022H Copyright © 2003-2020 Arm Limited or its affiliates. All rights reserved. A5-81
L2428@P80: ID040120 Non-Confidential
L2429@P80: A5.2 ID signals
L2430@P80: Each transaction channel has its own transaction ID. Table A5-1 shows these designated signals.
L2431@P80: Note
L2432@P80:  The AXI4 protocol supports an extended ordering model based on the use of the AXI ID transaction identifier. See
L2433@P80: Chapter A6 AXI Ordering Model.
L2434@P80: A5.2.1 Read data ordering
L2435@P80: The slave must ensure that the RID value of any returned data matches the ARID value of the address that it is
L2436@P80: responding to.
L2437@P80: The interconnect must ensure that the read data from a sequence of transactions with the same ARID value targeting
L2438@P80: different slaves is received by the master in the order that it issued the addresses.
L2439@P80: The read data reordering depth is the number of addresses pending in the slave that can be reordered. A slave that
L2440@P80: processes all transactions in order has a read data reordering depth of one. The read data reordering depth is a static
L2441@P80: value that must be specified by the designer of the slave.
L2442@P80: There is no mechanism that a master can use to determine the read data reordering depth of a slave.
L2443@P80: A5.2.2 Write data ordering
L2444@P80: A master must issue write data in the same order that it issues the transaction addresses.
L2445@P80: An interconnect that combines write transactions from different masters must ensure that it forwards the write data
L2446@P80: in address order.
L2447@P80: The interleaving of write data with different IDs was permitted in AXI3, but is deprecated in AXI4 and later. See
L2448@P80: the AMBA AXI and ACE Protocol Specification issue F specification for more details on write data interleaving.
L2449@P80: A5.2.3 Interconnect use of transaction identifiers
L2450@P80: When a master is connected to an interconnect, the interconnect appends additional bits to the ARID, AWID and
L2451@P80: WID identifiers that are unique to that master port. This has two effects:
L2452@P80: • Masters do not have to know what ID values are used by other masters because the interconnect makes the
L2453@P80: ID values used by each master unique by appending the master number to the original identifier.
L2454@P80: • The ID identifier at a slave interface is wider than the ID identifier at a master interface.
L2455@P80: For read data, the interconnect uses the additional bits of the RID identifier to determine which master port the read
L2456@P80: data is destined for. The interconnect removes these bits of the RID identifier before passing the RID value to the
L2457@P80: correct master port.
L2458@P80: Table A5-1 Channel transaction ID
L2459@P80: Transaction channel Transaction ID
L2460@P80: Write address channel AWID
L2461@P80: Write data channel, AXI3 only WIDa
L2462@P80: a. The WID signal is implemented only in AXI3.
L2463@P80: Write response channel BID
L2464@P80: Read address channel ARID
L2465@P80-81: Read data channel RIDA5 Transaction Identifiers
